{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>Shall the flocks and herds be slaughtered.</b> This is one of four things that Rabbi Akiva expounds and Rabbi Shimon does not expound as he does. Rabbi Akiva says: <b>Six hundred thousand men on foot, and You say, 'I will give them meat and they shall eat' — a month of days? Shall the flocks and herds be slaughtered?</b> — all at face value: what suffices for them? As in the manner of the verse (Leviticus 25) ״וּמָצָא כְדֵי גְאֻלָּתוֹ״. And which is harder — this, or 'Hear now, you rebels'? Rather, because he did not say it in public, Scripture spared him and did not punish him; but that of Merivah was in public, therefore Scripture did not spare him. Rabbi Shimon says: Heaven forbid that such a thing entered the mind of that righteous man — he of whom it is written ״בְּכָל בֵיתִי נֶאֱמָן הוּא״ (Numbers 12) — would say that the Omnipresent does not suffice for us? Rather, thus he said: <b>Six hundred thousand men on foot, etc., and You say, 'I will give meat'</b> for a month of days — and afterwards You will slaughter so great a nation as this? <b>Shall the flocks and herds be slaughtered for them</b> so that they are slaughtered and this food supply them forever? And is this your praise? It is like saying to a donkey: take a kor of barley and we will chop off your head! The Holy One, blessed be He, answered him: And if I do not give, they will say that My hand is too short — is it better in your eyes that the hand of HaShem appear short in their eyes? Let them perish, they and a hundred like them, rather than My hand be short before them even for one hour."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:10:27.587067721Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}